arXiv:2509.03341v1 [cs.LG] 03 Sep 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">On the MIA Vulnerability Gap Between Private GANs and Diffusion Models</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Ilana Sebag
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jean-Yves Franceschi
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Alain Rakotomamonjy
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Alexandre Allauzen
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jamal Atif
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) and diffusion models have emerged as leading approaches for high-quality image synthesis. While both can be trained under differential privacy (DP) to protect sensitive data, their sensitivity to membership inference attacks (MIAs), a key threat to data confidentiality, remains poorly understood. In this work, we present the first unified theoretical and empirical analysis of the privacy risks faced by differentially private generative models. We begin by showing, through a stability-based analysis, that GANs exhibit fundamentally lower sensitivity to data perturbations than diffusion models, suggesting a structural advantage in resisting MIAs. We then validate this insight with a comprehensive empirical study using a standardized MIA pipeline to evaluate privacy leakage across datasets and privacy budgets. Our results consistently reveal a marked privacy robustness gap in favor of GANs, even in strong DP regimes, highlighting that model type alone can critically shape privacy leakage.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="id1" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_align_bottom">
    
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><sup id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">1</sup> Criteo AI Lab, Paris, France</p>
<p id="p1.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><sup id="p1.2.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup> Miles Team, LAMSADE, Université Paris-Dauphine, PSL University, CNRS, Paris, France</p>
<p id="p1.3" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><sup id="p1.3.1" class="ltx_sup">3</sup> ESPCI PSL, Paris, France</p>
</div>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Generative models have become crucial in machine learning. Among leading generative architectures, GANs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goodfellow et al. 2014</a>)</cite> and diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho, Jain, and Abbeel 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al. 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karras et al. 2022</a>)</cite> dominate high-fidelity image synthesis. As these models are increasingly deployed in sensitive domains, their ability to memorize and reproduce training data raises serious privacy concerns, making protection against data leakage essential.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Differential Privacy (DP) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dwork et al. 2006</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dwork 2011</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dwork and Roth 2014</a>)</cite> provides a rigorous framework to mitigate this risk, ensuring that a model’s output is statistically indistinguishable when altering a single training data point. In practice, DP is commonly implemented via differentially private stochastic gradient descent (DP-SGD) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abadi et al. 2016</a>)</cite>, which clips per-sample gradients and adds calibrated noise during training.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">While both GANs and diffusion models can be trained with DP-SGD, their vulnerability to membership inference attacks (MIAs), which aim to determine whether a given sample was used during training, remains poorly understood <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shokri et al. 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carlini et al. 2022</a>)</cite>.
Moreover, empirical findings in the non-private setting suggest that GANs leak less membership information than diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carlini et al. 2023</a>)</cite>, raising a central yet unresolved question: <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">does this gap persist under formal privacy training, and if so, why</em>?</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we present the first unified theoretical and empirical study of membership leakage in differentially private generative models. To our knowledge, no prior work has analyzed how the training procedure affects data leakage under DP in the context of MIAs. We show in particular that DP-diffusion models are more vulnerable to such attacks than DP-GANs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Our analysis builds on the notion of uniform stability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bousquet and Elisseeff 2002</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hardt, Recht, and Singer 2016</a>)</cite>, which quantifies how much a model’s output changes when a single training point is replaced. We formally relate this stability to membership inference risk by bounding the adversarial advantage in terms of the model’s stability constant.
Crucially, we show that model’s stability is determined by its training dynamics. While DP-GANs apply DP-SGD only to the discriminator, diffusion models use DP-SGD to train a denoiser under a weighted multi-pass denoising objective. The main source of instability in diffusion models lies in the large loss weights assigned to low-noise denoising terms, which amplify the effect of small parameter changes. As a result, we prove that DP-diffusion models exhibit significantly lower stability and therefore leak more membership information under the same privacy budget.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We validate these insights empirically using a standardized evaluation pipeline. We train multiple instances of GANs and diffusion models in under comparable conditions, notably with the same DP-SGD mechanism and privacy budget <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, and conduct attacks using a consistent shadow-model framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shokri et al. 2017</a>)</cite>, relying on loss or logits based scoring in a black-box setting. Beyond validating the theory, this constitutes (to our knowledge) the first systematic assessment of membership leakage in differentially private generative models; prior work introducing DP-GANs and DP-diffusion has not assessed their vulnerability to membership inference.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Under identical privacy budgets, we observe consistent gaps in leakage between DP-GANs and DP-diffusion, indicating that the privacy parameter <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> alone does not fully characterize risk. Training architecture is a critical, yet often overlooked, factor of privacy leakage in differentially private generative models. Despite typically higher sample quality, diffusion models exhibit greater membership leakage under DP, whereas GANs are more robust, highlighting a trade-off between fidelity and privacy that has been largely overlooked. These results highlight the importance of evaluating private generative models not only in terms of output quality or reported <math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\varepsilon,\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\varepsilon,\delta)</annotation></semantics></math> values, but also through architecture-driven stability and empirical leakage metrics, which provide complementary insights into privacy risk.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Background and Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Notations.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the input space and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> the output space. Let <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=\{x_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m}\in\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=\{x_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m}\in\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> be the training dataset drawn i.i.d. from an unknown distribution <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>.
A learning algorithm is a map <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathcal{D}\to\mathcal{F}\subset\mathcal{Y}^{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathcal{D}\to\mathcal{F}\subset\mathcal{Y}^{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>, that assigns to each training set a hypothesis <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{D}\in\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{D}\in\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{D}:\mathcal{X}\to\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{D}:\mathcal{X}\to\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> is the learned model. We assume <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is symmetric with respect to the ordering of samples. For any <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,\dots,m\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,\dots,m\}</annotation></semantics></math>, we write <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\setminus i}=D\setminus\{x_{i}\}\in\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∖</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\setminus i}=D\setminus\{x_{i}\}\in\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> for the neighboring dataset obtained by removing one example. We denote by <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(f,z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(f,z)</annotation></semantics></math> the per-sample training loss incurred by model <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> on example <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>, and by <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{f}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{f}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> a scalar <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">attack score</em> computed from the model <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> on input <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Differential Privacy</h3>

<div id="Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A random mechanism <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}\colon\cal D\rightarrow\cal R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℛ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}\colon\cal D\rightarrow\cal R</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\varepsilon,\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\varepsilon,\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>-DP if for any two adjacent datasets <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D,D^{\prime}\in\cal D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝒟</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D,D^{\prime}\in\cal D</annotation></semantics></math> differing in at most one element and any for any subset of outputs <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}\subseteq\cal R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>ℛ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\subseteq\cal R</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}[\mathcal{M}(D)\in\mathcal{S}]\leq\mathrm{e}^{\varepsilon}\mathbb{P}[\mathcal{M}(D^{\prime})\in\mathcal{S}]+\delta." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>ε</mi></msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}[\mathcal{M}(D)\in\mathcal{S}]\leq\mathrm{e}^{\varepsilon}\mathbb{P}[\mathcal{M}(D^{\prime})\in\mathcal{S}]+\delta.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Adjacent inputs refer to datasets differing only by a single record. DP ensures that when a single record in a dataset is swapped, the change in the distribution of model outputs will be controlled by <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> controls the trade-off between the level of privacy and the usefulness of the output, where smaller <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> values offer stronger privacy but potentially lower utility (e.g. in our specific case, low-quality generated samples).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A classical example of a DP mechanism is the Gaussian mechanism operating on a function <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon\mathcal{D}\to{\mathbb{R}}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon\mathcal{D}\to{\mathbb{R}}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> as:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{M}_{f}(D)=\mathcal{N}(f(D),\sigma^{2}\mathcal{I}_{d})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{M}_{f}(D)=\mathcal{N}(f(D),\sigma^{2}\mathcal{I}_{d}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">We define the <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> sensitivity of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Delta_{2}(f):=\max_{D,D^{\prime}\text{: adjacent}\in\mathcal{D}}\|f(D)-f(D^{\prime})\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mtext>: adjacent</mtext><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{2}(f):=\max_{D,D^{\prime}\text{: adjacent}\in\mathcal{D}}\|f(D)-f(D^{\prime})\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{2}&gt;2\ln(1.25/\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1.25</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{2}&gt;2\ln(1.25/\delta)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\geq c\frac{\Delta_{2}(f)}{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>ε</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\geq c\frac{\Delta_{2}(f)}{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>, the Gaussian mechanism is <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\varepsilon,\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\varepsilon,\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>-DP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dwork and Roth 2014</a>)</cite>.
In deep learning, differential privacy is most commonly enforced using <em id="S2.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Differentially Private Stochastic Gradient Descent</em> (DP-SGD), introduced by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abadi et al. (2016)</a></cite>. The goal of DP-SGD is to ensure that each training example has a limited influence on the learned model. To achieve this, each training step involves computing per-sample gradients, clipping their norms to a fixed threshold, adding Gaussian noise, and performing a standard SGD update. This mechanism guarantees <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\varepsilon,\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\varepsilon,\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>-DP over the course of training, where the overall privacy loss is tracked using a composition accountant such as the moment accountant (introduced in the same work). The privacy budget <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> accumulates over iterations and depends on the batch size, number of steps, and the noise scale <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Differentially Private GANs and Diffusion Models</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Differential privacy has recently been applied to generative models, with most approaches relying on DP-SGD to perturb gradient updates during training. This subsection reviews how DP-SGD is integrated into two leading generative frameworks: GANs and diffusion models. In GANs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goodfellow et al. 2014</a>)</cite>, adversarial objectives are optimized under privacy constraints <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al. 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen, Orekondy, and Fritz 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Long et al. 2021</a>)</cite>, while diffusion models are adapted by injecting noise into gradient updates across denoising steps <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dockhorn et al. 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ghalebikesabi et al. 2023</a>)</cite>. However, how DP-SGD interacts with these training procedures and impacts privacy leakage remains poorly understood. We address this question through the lens of membership inference and algorithmic stability.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">GANs under Differential Privacy.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A GAN consists of a generator <math id="S2.SS2.SSSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\phi}(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\phi}(z)</annotation></semantics></math> that maps latent vectors <math id="S2.SS2.SSSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\sim p_{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>z</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\sim p_{z}</annotation></semantics></math> to samples, and a discriminator <math id="S2.SS2.SSSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\psi}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\psi}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> that distinguishes real from generated data. In our setting, <math id="S2.SS2.SSSx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\psi}(x)\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\psi}(x)\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> returns a logit, with the sigmoid <math id="S2.SS2.SSSx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(u)=1/(1+e^{-u})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(u)=1/(1+e^{-u})</annotation></semantics></math> applied inside the loss function. Their parameters <math id="S2.SS2.SSSx1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSSx1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> are optimized through the following minmax objective:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{\phi}\max_{\psi}\ " display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>ψ</mi></munder></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{\phi}\max_{\psi}\ </annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{x\sim p_{\text{data}}}[\log\sigma(D_{\psi}(x))]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{x\sim p_{\text{data}}}[\log\sigma(D_{\psi}(x))]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\mathbb{E}_{z\sim p_{z}}[\log(1-\sigma(D_{\psi}(G_{\phi}(z))))]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>z</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\mathbb{E}_{z\sim p_{z}}[\log(1-\sigma(D_{\psi}(G_{\phi}(z))))]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the DP setting, only the discriminator accesses real data and is trained with DP-SGD. The generator receives updates exclusively through backpropagation from the discriminator, and thus qualifies as post-processing. As a result, its updates incur no additional privacy cost. This decoupling is key to the relative robustness of GANs under DP, and is further explored in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bie, Kamath, and Zhang (2023)</a></cite>, which proposes techniques for stabilizing GAN training in this regime.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Diffusion Models under Differential Privacy.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song and Ermon 2020</a>)</cite> synthesize data by reversing a stochastic process that gradually corrupts clean images with Gaussian noise. Given a sample <math id="S2.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}\sim p_{\text{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}\sim p_{\text{data}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the forward process generates noisy inputs <math id="S2.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> as: <math id="S2.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\sigma}=x_{0}+\sigma\epsilon,\quad\epsilon\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\mathcal{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\sigma}=x_{0}+\sigma\epsilon,\quad\epsilon\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\mathcal{I})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in[\sigma_{\min},\sigma_{\max}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in[\sigma_{\min},\sigma_{\max}]</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the noise level. The model <math id="S2.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{\sigma},\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{\sigma},\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> is trained to predict <math id="S2.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> using a denoising loss. Following the EDM formulation of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karras et al. (2022)</a></cite>, the training loss is expressed as follows:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{x_{0},\sigma,\epsilon}\left[\lambda(\sigma)\cdot\left\|\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{0}+\sigma\epsilon,\sigma)-\epsilon\right\|^{2}\right]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{x_{0},\sigma,\epsilon}\left[\lambda(\sigma)\cdot\left\|\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{0}+\sigma\epsilon,\sigma)-\epsilon\right\|^{2}\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSSx2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the EDM weighting function <math id="S2.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda(\sigma)=\frac{\sigma^{2}+\sigma_{\text{data}}^{2}}{(\sigma\cdot\sigma_{\text{data}})^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mtext>data</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda(\sigma)=\frac{\sigma^{2}+\sigma_{\text{data}}^{2}}{(\sigma\cdot\sigma_{\text{data}})^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> reweights contributions across noise levels. In the DP setting, Equation <a href="#S2.E4" title="In Diffusion Models under Differential Privacy. ‣ 2.2 Differentially Private GANs and Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Background and Related Work ‣ On the MIA Vulnerability Gap Between Private GANs and Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> is approximated by sampling <math id="S2.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> independent noise levels <math id="S2.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\sigma_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\sigma_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math> and corresponding <math id="S2.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> for each training example, resulting in a Monte Carlo approximation. This formulation, referred to as the <em id="S2.SS2.SSSx2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">noise multiplicity</em> approach by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dockhorn et al. (2023)</a></cite>, takes the form:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}\lambda(\sigma_{k})\cdot\left\|\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{0}+\sigma_{k}\epsilon_{k},\sigma_{k})-\epsilon_{k}\right\|^{2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}\lambda(\sigma_{k})\cdot\left\|\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{0}+\sigma_{k}\epsilon_{k},\sigma_{k})-\epsilon_{k}\right\|^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSSx2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Each training example contributes <math id="S2.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> noise-conditioned loss evaluations per step. The per-sample gradients are averaged to reduce variance which must be accounted for in DP-SGD.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Membership Inference Attacks</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Membership inference attacks (MIAs) aim to determine whether a particular data point was used to train a machine learning model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shokri et al. 2017</a>)</cite>. Given a model <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> trained on a dataset <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> and a sample <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, the adversary <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> attempts to infer whether <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in D</annotation></semantics></math> (a <em id="S2.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">member</em>) or <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\notin D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\notin D</annotation></semantics></math> (a <em id="S2.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">non-member</em>). Formally, the attack is framed as a binary decision function:</p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}:x\mapsto\mathcal{C}(s_{f}(x))\in\{0,1\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}:x\mapsto\mathcal{C}(s_{f}(x))\in\{0,1\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is a classifier, <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{f}(x)\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{f}(x)\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is a scalar <em id="S2.SS3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">attack score</em> extracted from the model’s behavior on input <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. The attack score, which is model-specific, quantifies the model’s confidence or sensitivity on input <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and is used by the attacker to infer membership via a classifier <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. In GAN-based attacks, it is often computed from the discriminator’s raw logit, e.g. <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{f}(x)=D_{\psi}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{f}(x)=D_{\psi}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. 2020</a>)</cite>, which reflects the discriminator’s confidence that <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is real.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In diffusion models, the attack score is typically the scalar denoising loss: <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{f}(x)=\mathbb{E}_{\epsilon,\sigma}\left\|\epsilon_{\theta}(x+\sigma\epsilon,\sigma)-\epsilon\right\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{f}(x)=\mathbb{E}_{\epsilon,\sigma}\left\|\epsilon_{\theta}(x+\sigma\epsilon,\sigma)-\epsilon\right\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carlini et al. 2023</a>)</cite>,
which measures how well the model reconstructs noisy versions of <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. In both cases, members tend to have different scores (higher confidence or lower reconstruction error), enabling the attacker to distinguish them from non-members.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To quantify the effectiveness of such MIAs, we use the following definition of attacker advantage.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Attacker advantage, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yeom et al. (2018)</a></cite>)<span id="Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The attacker advantage quantifies the gap between true and false positive rates in membership inference:</p>
<table id="S2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{ADV}_{\text{MIA}}=\mathbb{P}[\mathcal{A}(x)=1\mid x\in D]-\mathbb{P}[\mathcal{A}(x)=1\mid x\notin D]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ADV</mtext><mtext>MIA</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{ADV}_{\text{MIA}}=\mathbb{P}[\mathcal{A}(x)=1\mid x\in D]-\mathbb{P}[\mathcal{A}(x)=1\mid x\notin D],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is the attacker’s decision on whether <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is in the training set. A value of <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{ADV}_{\text{MIA}}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ADV</mtext><mtext>MIA</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{ADV}_{\text{MIA}}=0</annotation></semantics></math> indicates perfect privacy: the attacker performs no better than random guessing. Higher values reflect greater privacy leakage, as the attacker can better distinguish training from non-training samples.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A key factor enabling MIAs is the behavioral gap between training and unseen data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shokri et al. 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yeom et al. 2018</a>)</cite>. A standard approach to exploit this gap is <em id="S2.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">shadow modeling</em>, where the adversary trains auxiliary models on disjoint datasets with known membership labels to mimic the target model’s behavior. These shadow models generate scores used to train a membership classifier <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> that learns to distinguish members from non-members. Originally introduced in the black-box setting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shokri et al. 2017</a>)</cite>, shadow modeling has since been adapted to scenarios where the attacker has partial knowledge of the target’s architecture or training procedure <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nasr, Shokri, and Houmansadr 2019</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To better understand what drives membership leakage, we now turn to the notion of algorithmic stability.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4 </span>Algorithmic Stability</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Algorithmic stability measures how much a learning algorithm’s output changes in response to small perturbations in the training data. It is a classical tool for understanding generalization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bousquet and Elisseeff 2002</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hardt, Recht, and Singer 2016</a>)</cite>, and more recently, it has emerged as a key concept in quantifying privacy leakage.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the context of membership inference, the link is intuitive: if a membership inference attack succeeds on a model, then its predictions must change noticeably between training and unseen examples. This suggests that small changes to the training data, like removing a single example, can influence the model’s output. In contrast, a model whose predictions remain consistent when the data is slightly modified is more likely to resist such attacks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To analyze the stability of a learning algorithm that maps a dataset to a function <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> (which may represent the full model or a specific component trained by the algorithm), we require a metric to evaluate the quality of the function’s output. We therefore define a loss function <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(f,z)\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(f,z)\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a data sample. Depending on the setting, <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> can be either <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math>, in which case <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(f,z)=c(f(x),y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(f,z)=c(f(x),y)</annotation></semantics></math>, or <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=(x)</annotation></semantics></math>,
in which case <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(f,z)=c(f(x),x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(f,z)=c(f(x),x)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(\cdot,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(\cdot,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is a cost function.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Uniform stability, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bousquet and Elisseeff (2002)</a></cite>)<span id="Thmdefinition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The function <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="Thmdefinition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-uniformly stable</em> with respect to a loss function <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> if, for any training set <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> of size <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, and any index <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S2.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|\ell(f_{D},\cdot)-\ell(f_{D^{\setminus i}},\cdot)\right\|_{\infty}\leq\beta." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∖</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|\ell(f_{D},\cdot)-\ell(f_{D^{\setminus i}},\cdot)\right\|_{\infty}\leq\beta.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{D}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{D^{\setminus i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∖</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{D^{\setminus i}}</annotation></semantics></math> are hypothesis functions obtained
by training the algorithm with respectively dataset <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\setminus i}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∖</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\setminus i}.</annotation></semantics></math> Uniform stability quantifies how sensitive a learning algorithm is to changes in a single training point.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Recent work has formalized the link between stability and privacy attacks. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yeom et al. (2018)</a></cite> show that high empirical advantage in a membership inference attack implies instability of the learning algorithm, and vice versa. Moreover, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carlini et al. (2022)</a></cite> further argue that instability is often exacerbated in overparameterized or poorly regularized models (conditions that commonly arise in generative modeling). These insights motivate the use of stability analysis as a tool for explaining privacy leakage.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we use uniform stability to compare the privacy properties of differentially private GANs and diffusion models. By analyzing how their outputs react to the removal of a single training point, we derive theoretical bounds on membership advantage.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Stability–Based Analysis of MIA Risk in DP GANs and DP Diffusion Models</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Empirically, non-private GANs leak less membership information than diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carlini et al. 2023</a>)</cite>. We provide a formal theoretical explanation grounded in <em id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">uniform stability</em>, comparing the GANs and Diffusion models in the private setting.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We proceed in three steps. First, we show that the attack scores are Lipschitz with respect to the training loss (Props. <a href="#Thmproperty1" title="Property 1 (Loss–score Lipschitz link for GANs). ‣ 3.1 Linking Uniform Stability to Attack Scores ‣ 3 Stability–Based Analysis of MIA Risk in DP GANs and DP Diffusion Models ‣ On the MIA Vulnerability Gap Between Private GANs and Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, <a href="#Thmproperty2" title="Property 2 (Loss–score Lipschitz link for diffusion models). ‣ 3.1 Linking Uniform Stability to Attack Scores ‣ 3 Stability–Based Analysis of MIA Risk in DP GANs and DP Diffusion Models ‣ On the MIA Vulnerability Gap Between Private GANs and Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), implying that loss stability transfers to score stability (Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma1" title="Lemma 1 (Score stability). ‣ 3.1 Linking Uniform Stability to Attack Scores ‣ 3 Stability–Based Analysis of MIA Risk in DP GANs and DP Diffusion Models ‣ On the MIA Vulnerability Gap Between Private GANs and Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). Second, under a bounded score density, score stability bounds the membership advantage of any threshold attack (Thm. <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Bound on membership advantage under uniform score stability). ‣ 3.2 Stability Bound on Membership Advantage ‣ 3 Stability–Based Analysis of MIA Risk in DP GANs and DP Diffusion Models ‣ On the MIA Vulnerability Gap Between Private GANs and Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). Third, we derive a general DP-SGD stability bound (Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma2" title="Lemma 2 (Uniform stability of functions trained by DP-SGD). ‣ 3.3 Uniform Stability of Functions Trained by DP-SGD ‣ 3 Stability–Based Analysis of MIA Risk in DP GANs and DP Diffusion Models ‣ On the MIA Vulnerability Gap Between Private GANs and Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) and instantiate it for GANs and diffusion (Lemmas <a href="#Thmlemma3" title="Lemma 3 (Stability bound for DP-GANs). ‣ 3.4 Model-Specific Stability Bounds ‣ 3 Stability–Based Analysis of MIA Risk in DP GANs and DP Diffusion Models ‣ On the MIA Vulnerability Gap Between Private GANs and Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, <a href="#Thmlemma4" title="Lemma 4 (Stability bound for DP-diffusion models). ‣ 3.4 Model-Specific Stability Bounds ‣ 3 Stability–Based Analysis of MIA Risk in DP GANs and DP Diffusion Models ‣ On the MIA Vulnerability Gap Between Private GANs and Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) to compare privacy leakage between both models.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Linking Uniform Stability to Attack Scores</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Uniform stability (Definition <a href="#Thmdefinition3" title="Definition 3 (Uniform stability, ( ) ). ‣ 2.4 Algorithmic Stability ‣ 2 Background and Related Work ‣ On the MIA Vulnerability Gap Between Private GANs and Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) bounds the <em id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">loss</em> drift when a single training point is removed. To translate this into a bound on any <em id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">attack score</em>, we introduce a regularity property connecting the training loss and the attack score. This property enables us to formally relate the algorithm’s training stability to the success of an MIA.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproperty1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_property">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproperty1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Property 1</span></span><span id="Thmproperty1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Loss–score Lipschitz link for GANs)<span id="Thmproperty1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproperty1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproperty1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproperty1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmproperty1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=D_{\psi}\in\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{GAN}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>GAN</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=D_{\psi}\in\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{GAN}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a discriminator parameterized by <math id="Thmproperty1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>, trained using the logistic loss. For any input <math id="Thmproperty1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and label <math id="Thmproperty1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\{-1,+1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\{-1,+1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, define:</span></p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The </span><em id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph">score</em><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> used by the attacker is the raw logit: </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{f}(x):=D_{\psi}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{f}(x):=D_{\psi}(x)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The </span><em id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph">training loss</em><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is the logistic loss: </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(f,x,y):=\log(1+e^{-y\,s_{f}(x)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(f,x,y):=\log(1+e^{-y\,s_{f}(x)})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="Thmproperty1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproperty1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproperty1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume the loss values lie in a compact interval <math id="Thmproperty1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]\subset\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]\subset\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the map <math id="Thmproperty1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\mapsto s_{f}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\mapsto s_{f}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is Lipschitz with respect to <math id="Thmproperty1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(f,x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(f,x,y)</annotation></semantics></math>, with</span></p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|s_{f}(x)-s_{f^{\prime}}(x)|\leq L_{s}\cdot|\ell(f,x,y)-\ell(f^{\prime},x,y)|," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|s_{f}(x)-s_{f^{\prime}}(x)|\leq L_{s}\cdot|\ell(f,x,y)-\ell(f^{\prime},x,y)|,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmproperty1.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproperty1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmproperty1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{s}=\sup_{u\in[a,b]}\frac{e^{u}}{e^{u}-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>u</mi></msup><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>u</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{s}=\sup_{u\in[a,b]}\frac{e^{u}}{e^{u}-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">See Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Proofs of Properties and ‣ On the MIA Vulnerability Gap Between Private GANs and Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmproperty2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_property">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproperty2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Property 2</span></span><span id="Thmproperty2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Loss–score Lipschitz link for diffusion models)<span id="Thmproperty2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproperty2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproperty2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproperty2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmproperty2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=\epsilon_{\theta}\in\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{Diff}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>Diff</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=\epsilon_{\theta}\in\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{Diff}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a denoising network parameterized by <math id="Thmproperty2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, trained using the EDM objective (Equation <a href="#A1.E51" title="In 2nd item ‣ Property 2 (Loss–score Lipschitz link for diffusion models). ‣ Appendix A Proofs of Properties and ‣ On the MIA Vulnerability Gap Between Private GANs and Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">51</span></a>) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karras et al. 2022</a>)</cite>. Define:</span></p>
<ul id="S3.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the </span><em id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph">attack score</em><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> as the scalar denoising error:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{f}(x):=\mathbb{E}_{\epsilon,\sigma}\left\|\epsilon_{\theta}(x+\sigma\epsilon,\sigma)-\epsilon\right\|^{2};" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{f}(x):=\mathbb{E}_{\epsilon,\sigma}\left\|\epsilon_{\theta}(x+\sigma\epsilon,\sigma)-\epsilon\right\|^{2};</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the </span><em id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph">training loss</em><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> as the noise-weighted EDM objective:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(f,x):=\mathbb{E}_{\epsilon,\sigma}\left[\lambda(\sigma)\cdot\left\|\epsilon_{\theta}(x+\sigma\epsilon,\sigma)-\epsilon\right\|^{2}\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(f,x):=\mathbb{E}_{\epsilon,\sigma}\left[\lambda(\sigma)\cdot\left\|\epsilon_{\theta}(x+\sigma\epsilon,\sigma)-\epsilon\right\|^{2}\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where </span><math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda(\sigma)\in[\lambda_{\min},\lambda_{\max}]\subset(0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda(\sigma)\in[\lambda_{\min},\lambda_{\max}]\subset(0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a bounded weighting function.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="Thmproperty2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproperty2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then, for any <math id="Thmproperty2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f,f^{\prime}\in\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{Diff}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>Diff</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f,f^{\prime}\in\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{Diff}}</annotation></semantics></math> and any <math id="Thmproperty2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, the following inequality holds:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|s_{f}(x)-s_{f^{\prime}}(x)|\leq\frac{1}{\lambda_{\min}}\cdot\left\|\ell(f,\cdot)-\ell(f^{\prime},\cdot)\right\|_{\infty}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|s_{f}(x)-s_{f^{\prime}}(x)|\leq\frac{1}{\lambda_{\min}}\cdot\left\|\ell(f,\cdot)-\ell(f^{\prime},\cdot)\right\|_{\infty}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmproperty2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproperty2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">That is, the attack score is <math id="Thmproperty2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\min}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\min}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz with respect to the training loss.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">See in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Proofs of Properties and ‣ On the MIA Vulnerability Gap Between Private GANs and Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The following lemma generalizes the score–loss relationships from Properties <a href="#Thmproperty1" title="Property 1 (Loss–score Lipschitz link for GANs). ‣ 3.1 Linking Uniform Stability to Attack Scores ‣ 3 Stability–Based Analysis of MIA Risk in DP GANs and DP Diffusion Models ‣ On the MIA Vulnerability Gap Between Private GANs and Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#Thmproperty2" title="Property 2 (Loss–score Lipschitz link for diffusion models). ‣ 3.1 Linking Uniform Stability to Attack Scores ‣ 3 Stability–Based Analysis of MIA Risk in DP GANs and DP Diffusion Models ‣ On the MIA Vulnerability Gap Between Private GANs and Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, yielding a stability bound on attack scores from the uniform stability of the training loss.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1</span></span><span id="Thmlemma1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Score stability)<span id="Thmlemma1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If the learning algorithm is <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-uniformly stable with respect to the loss <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, and the attack score function satisfies Properties <a href="#Thmproperty1" title="Property 1 (Loss–score Lipschitz link for GANs). ‣ 3.1 Linking Uniform Stability to Attack Scores ‣ 3 Stability–Based Analysis of MIA Risk in DP GANs and DP Diffusion Models ‣ On the MIA Vulnerability Gap Between Private GANs and Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>,<a href="#Thmproperty2" title="Property 2 (Loss–score Lipschitz link for diffusion models). ‣ 3.1 Linking Uniform Stability to Attack Scores ‣ 3 Stability–Based Analysis of MIA Risk in DP GANs and DP Diffusion Models ‣ On the MIA Vulnerability Gap Between Private GANs and Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, then for all <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|s_{f_{D}}(x)-s_{f_{D^{\setminus i}}}(x)|\leq L_{s}\cdot\beta." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∖</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|s_{f_{D}}(x)-s_{f_{D^{\setminus i}}}(x)|\leq L_{s}\cdot\beta.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Immediate by Lipschitz continuity of <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\cdot}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>⋅</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\cdot}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> from Properties <a href="#Thmproperty1" title="Property 1 (Loss–score Lipschitz link for GANs). ‣ 3.1 Linking Uniform Stability to Attack Scores ‣ 3 Stability–Based Analysis of MIA Risk in DP GANs and DP Diffusion Models ‣ On the MIA Vulnerability Gap Between Private GANs and Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>,<a href="#Thmproperty2" title="Property 2 (Loss–score Lipschitz link for diffusion models). ‣ 3.1 Linking Uniform Stability to Attack Scores ‣ 3 Stability–Based Analysis of MIA Risk in DP GANs and DP Diffusion Models ‣ On the MIA Vulnerability Gap Between Private GANs and Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Stability Bound on Membership Advantage</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Uniform stability limits how much a model’s behavior can change when a single training point is removed, making it harder for an adversary to distinguish members from non-members. While <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yeom et al. (2018)</a></cite> showed that uniform stability bounds the membership advantage of threshold attacks based directly on the loss, our result extends this guarantee to a broader class of attacks. Specifically, we prove in Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Bound on membership advantage under uniform score stability). ‣ 3.2 Stability Bound on Membership Advantage ‣ 3 Stability–Based Analysis of MIA Risk in DP GANs and DP Diffusion Models ‣ On the MIA Vulnerability Gap Between Private GANs and Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> that any threshold-based adversary using a score function that is Lipschitz-continuous with respect to the loss, such as discriminator logits or denoising errors, also yields bounded membership advantage. This provides a new theoretical guarantee that captures more realistic attack settings beyond loss-based inference.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Bound on membership advantage under uniform score stability)<span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> be a learning algorithm that is <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-uniformly stable with respect to a loss function <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, and suppose the loss–score Lipschitz condition holds with constant <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{s}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{s}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> (Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma1" title="Lemma 1 (Score stability). ‣ 3.1 Linking Uniform Stability to Attack Scores ‣ 3 Stability–Based Analysis of MIA Risk in DP GANs and DP Diffusion Models ‣ On the MIA Vulnerability Gap Between Private GANs and Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). Assume further that the distribution of the score <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{f_{D}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{f_{D}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> admits a bounded density with upper bound <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, for any threshold-based adversary of the form</span></p>
<table id="S3.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{A}(x)=\mathbb{I}\{s_{f_{D}}(x)\leq\tau\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝕀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}(x)=\mathbb{I}\{s_{f_{D}}(x)\leq\tau\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the membership advantage is bounded as</span></p>
<table id="S3.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{ADV}_{\texttt{MIA}}\;\leq\;2QL_{s}\beta." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ADV</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">MIA</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mrow><mn> 2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{ADV}_{\texttt{MIA}}\;\leq\;2QL_{s}\beta.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">See Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Proof of Theorem 1 ‣ On the MIA Vulnerability Gap Between Private GANs and Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The bound in Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Bound on membership advantage under uniform score stability). ‣ 3.2 Stability Bound on Membership Advantage ‣ 3 Stability–Based Analysis of MIA Risk in DP GANs and DP Diffusion Models ‣ On the MIA Vulnerability Gap Between Private GANs and Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is informative only when <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2QL_{s}\beta&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2QL_{s}\beta&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, since by definition <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{ADV}_{\texttt{MIA}}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ADV</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">MIA</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{ADV}_{\texttt{MIA}}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. This condition imposes a constraint on <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{s}\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{s}\beta</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> decreases with the dataset size <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>; for example, standard bounds for DP-SGD with per-sample gradient clipping yield <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=\mathcal{O}(1/m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=\mathcal{O}(1/m)</annotation></semantics></math>, making the bound tighter as <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> increases (see Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma2" title="Lemma 2 (Uniform stability of functions trained by DP-SGD). ‣ 3.3 Uniform Stability of Functions Trained by DP-SGD ‣ 3 Stability–Based Analysis of MIA Risk in DP GANs and DP Diffusion Models ‣ On the MIA Vulnerability Gap Between Private GANs and Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> for a formal derivation of this bound). Conversely, <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> quantifies the sensitivity of the attack score to changes in the loss, and is specific to the model and chosen score function. Overall, the tightness of the bound reflects a trade-off between algorithmic stability and the score’s sensitivity to perturbations. We provide a more detailed discussion in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Additional Discussion on Theorem ‣ On the MIA Vulnerability Gap Between Private GANs and Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Uniform Stability of Functions Trained by DP-SGD</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To understand the behaviour of the MIA advantage bound from Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Bound on membership advantage under uniform score stability). ‣ 3.2 Stability Bound on Membership Advantage ‣ 3 Stability–Based Analysis of MIA Risk in DP GANs and DP Diffusion Models ‣ On the MIA Vulnerability Gap Between Private GANs and Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, it is crucial to characterize the uniform stability parameter <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, our analysis relies on the fact that <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=\mathcal{O}(1/m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=\mathcal{O}(1/m)</annotation></semantics></math>, a property we now formalize. We derive a general upper bound on the expected uniform stability of functions trained by DP-SGD, which we later instantiate for GAN discriminators and diffusion denoisers.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2</span></span><span id="Thmlemma2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Uniform stability of functions trained by DP-SGD)<span id="Thmlemma2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(f_{\theta},z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(f_{\theta},z)</annotation></semantics></math> be a loss that is <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz in the parameters <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, for all <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose DP-SGD runs for <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> steps with per-sample gradient clipping at norm <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. At each step <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, a mini-batch <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> of constant size <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> is sampled uniformly without replacement from a dataset of size <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, and a learning rate <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is applied. Then, uniform stability of functions trained by DP-SGD is defined as follows:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\;:=\;\sup_{z,\,i}\;\mathbb{E}\!\left[\,\big|\ell(f_{D},z)-\ell(f_{D^{\setminus i}},z)\big|\,\right]\;\leq\;\frac{2LC}{m}\,\sum_{t=1}^{T}\alpha_{t}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.558em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi>𝔼</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em">[</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∖</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.170em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.280em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\;:=\;\sup_{z,\,i}\;\mathbb{E}\!\left[\,\big|\ell(f_{D},z)-\ell(f_{D^{\setminus i}},z)\big|\,\right]\;\leq\;\frac{2LC}{m}\,\sum_{t=1}^{T}\alpha_{t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmlemma2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular, for constant step size <math id="Thmlemma2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}=\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}=\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, we have:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\leq\frac{2LC\alpha T}{m}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\leq\frac{2LC\alpha T}{m}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Notice that we consider two neighboring datasets <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\setminus i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∖</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\setminus i}</annotation></semantics></math>, differing in a single example, and analyze two executions of DP-SGD that are coupled via shared randomness, that is, they use the same sequence of mini-batches and the same Gaussian noise vectors. This coupling isolates the effect of the data perturbation from that of the stochastic noise. Consequently, the bound reflects the sensitivity of the algorithm rather than the effect of noise, which is why the DP noise scale <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> does not appear explicitly in the stability bound (More details in Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Additional Comments on Common Randomness (Lemma 2) ‣ On the MIA Vulnerability Gap Between Private GANs and Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{t}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{t}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the parameter vectors at step <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> of two models trained with DP-SGD on <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\setminus i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∖</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\setminus i}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. At each step, we sample a mini-batch <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> of size <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> and perform the update:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{t+1}\leftarrow\theta_{t}-\alpha_{t}\left(\frac{1}{|B_{t}|}\sum_{j\in B_{t}}\text{clip}(\nabla\ell(f_{\theta_{t}};z_{j}),C)+\eta_{t}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mtext>clip</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{t+1}\leftarrow\theta_{t}-\alpha_{t}\left(\frac{1}{|B_{t}|}\sum_{j\in B_{t}}\text{clip}(\nabla\ell(f_{\theta_{t}};z_{j}),C)+\eta_{t}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2}I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2}I)</annotation></semantics></math>, and similarly for <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{t}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{t}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">At step <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the update uses the <em id="S3.SS3.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">mean</em> of clipped per-sample gradients. If <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is not in the mini-batch, the parameter updates for <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{t}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{t}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> coincide; if it is, the mean changes by at most <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C/b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C/b</annotation></semantics></math> in norm because clipping ensures each per-sample contribution has norm <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq C</annotation></semantics></math>. The event “<math id="S3.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is in the batch” occurs with probability <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b/m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b/m</annotation></semantics></math>, when the differing sample is included in the mini-batch. Hence the <em id="S3.SS3.p4.1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">expected</em> change in the update (in norm) at step <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is at most <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(b/m)\cdot(C/b)=C/m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(b/m)\cdot(C/b)=C/m</annotation></semantics></math>. Multiplying by the step size <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, we get</span></p>
<table id="S3.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\big[\|\theta_{t+1}-\theta_{t+1}^{\prime}\|\big]\;\leq\;\mathbb{E}\big[\|\theta_{t}-\theta_{t}^{\prime}\|\big]\;+\;\alpha_{t}\,\frac{C}{m}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.280em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.280em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mi>C</mi><mi>m</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\big[\|\theta_{t+1}-\theta_{t+1}^{\prime}\|\big]\;\leq\;\mathbb{E}\big[\|\theta_{t}-\theta_{t}^{\prime}\|\big]\;+\;\alpha_{t}\,\frac{C}{m}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">Therefore, it yields that
<math id="S3.SS3.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\big[\|\theta_{T}-\theta_{T}^{\prime}\|\big]\leq\frac{C}{m}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\alpha_{t}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>C</mi><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\big[\|\theta_{T}-\theta_{T}^{\prime}\|\big]\leq\frac{C}{m}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\alpha_{t}.</annotation></semantics></math>
Since <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\cdot;z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\cdot;z)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz in <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, we conclude</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\!\left[\,\big|\ell(f_{D},z)-\ell(f_{D^{\setminus i}},z)\big|\,\right]\;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi>𝔼</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em">[</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∖</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.170em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\!\left[\,\big|\ell(f_{D},z)-\ell(f_{D^{\setminus i}},z)\big|\,\right]\;</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\;L\,\mathbb{E}\big[\|\theta_{T}-\theta_{T}^{\prime}\|\big]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\;L\,\mathbb{E}\big[\|\theta_{T}-\theta_{T}^{\prime}\|\big]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\;\frac{LC}{m}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\alpha_{t}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\;\frac{LC}{m}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\alpha_{t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Model-Specific Stability Bounds</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We apply Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma2" title="Lemma 2 (Uniform stability of functions trained by DP-SGD). ‣ 3.3 Uniform Stability of Functions Trained by DP-SGD ‣ 3 Stability–Based Analysis of MIA Risk in DP GANs and DP Diffusion Models ‣ On the MIA Vulnerability Gap Between Private GANs and Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> to the two generative families studied here. Our bounds have the same structure and differ through the loss Lipschitz constants and total update counts.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3</span></span><span id="Thmlemma3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Stability bound for DP-GANs)<span id="Thmlemma3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmlemma3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\psi}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the discriminator of a GAN, trained with DP-SGD over <math id="Thmlemma3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> steps with per-sample clipping at norm <math id="Thmlemma3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and learning rates <math id="Thmlemma3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\alpha_{t}\}_{t=1}^{T_{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\alpha_{t}\}_{t=1}^{T_{G}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume the discriminator score <math id="Thmlemma3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\psi}(x):=D_{\psi}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\psi}(x):=D_{\psi}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmlemma3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz in parameters <math id="Thmlemma3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>, and define the logistic loss</span></p>
<table id="S3.E22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{G}(\psi;x,y):=\log\!\big(1+e^{-y\,s_{\psi}(x)}\big),\quad y\in\{-1,+1\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.146em;" width="1.146em"><mi>log</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{G}(\psi;x,y):=\log\!\big(1+e^{-y\,s_{\psi}(x)}\big),\quad y\in\{-1,+1\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmlemma3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then the expected uniform stability of the DP-GAN discriminator satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S3.E23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\mathrm{GAN}}\;\leq\;\frac{2LC}{m}\sum_{t=1}^{T_{G}}\alpha_{t}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>GAN</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></munderover><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\mathrm{GAN}}\;\leq\;\frac{2LC}{m}\sum_{t=1}^{T_{G}}\alpha_{t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">The function <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\mapsto\log(1+e^{-yz})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\mapsto\log(1+e^{-yz})</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz in <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\{-1,+1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\{-1,+1\}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\psi}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\psi}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz in <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>, then by the composition of Lipschitz functions, the loss <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{G}(\psi;x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{G}(\psi;x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz in <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>. That is,</span></p>
<table id="S3.E24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\ell_{G}(\psi;x,y)-\ell_{G}(\psi^{\prime};x,y)|\leq L\,\|\psi-\psi^{\prime}\|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\ell_{G}(\psi;x,y)-\ell_{G}(\psi^{\prime};x,y)|\leq L\,\|\psi-\psi^{\prime}\|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">Applying Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma2" title="Lemma 2 (Uniform stability of functions trained by DP-SGD). ‣ 3.3 Uniform Stability of Functions Trained by DP-SGD ‣ 3 Stability–Based Analysis of MIA Risk in DP GANs and DP Diffusion Models ‣ On the MIA Vulnerability Gap Between Private GANs and Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> with Lipschitz constant <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{G}\leq L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{G}\leq L</annotation></semantics></math>, dataset size <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, and total number of steps <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=T_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=T_{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, the uniform stability satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S3.E25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\mathrm{GAN}}\;\leq\;\frac{2L_{G}C}{m}\sum_{t=1}^{T_{G}}\alpha_{t}\;\leq\;\frac{2LC}{m}\sum_{t=1}^{T_{G}}\alpha_{t}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>GAN</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></munderover><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></munderover><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\mathrm{GAN}}\;\leq\;\frac{2L_{G}C}{m}\sum_{t=1}^{T_{G}}\alpha_{t}\;\leq\;\frac{2LC}{m}\sum_{t=1}^{T_{G}}\alpha_{t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4</span></span><span id="Thmlemma4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Stability bound for DP-diffusion models)<span id="Thmlemma4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmlemma4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> be a denoiser trained with DP-SGD over <math id="Thmlemma4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{D}</annotation></semantics></math> steps using the multi-pass EDM loss:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{D}(\theta;x,y):=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}\lambda(\sigma_{k})\,\big\|\epsilon_{\theta}(x+\sigma_{k}\epsilon_{k},\sigma_{k},y)-\epsilon_{k}\big\|^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{D}(\theta;x,y):=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}\lambda(\sigma_{k})\,\big\|\epsilon_{\theta}(x+\sigma_{k}\epsilon_{k},\sigma_{k},y)-\epsilon_{k}\big\|^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmlemma4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmlemma4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{k}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{k}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmlemma4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda(\sigma_{k}):=\frac{\sigma_{k}^{2}+\sigma_{\mathrm{data}}^{2}}{(\sigma_{k}\sigma_{\mathrm{data}})^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>data</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>data</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda(\sigma_{k}):=\frac{\sigma_{k}^{2}+\sigma_{\mathrm{data}}^{2}}{(\sigma_{k}\sigma_{\mathrm{data}})^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> are fixed weights. Assume that the prediction error is uniformly bounded: <math id="Thmlemma4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\epsilon_{\theta}(x+\sigma_{k}\epsilon_{k},\sigma_{k},y)-\epsilon_{k}\|\leq B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\epsilon_{\theta}(x+\sigma_{k}\epsilon_{k},\sigma_{k},y)-\epsilon_{k}\|\leq B</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="Thmlemma4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and all <math id="Thmlemma4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, and that the denoiser <math id="Thmlemma4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmlemma4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz in <math id="Thmlemma4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> for fixed input.
Then the per-sample training loss <math id="Thmlemma4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{D}(\theta;x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{D}(\theta;x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> is Lipschitz in <math id="Thmlemma4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> with constant</span></p>
<table id="S3.E27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{D}\leq 2\,\bar{\lambda}\,L\,B,\&gt;\&gt;\&gt;\text{where }\bar{\lambda}:=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}\lambda(\sigma_{k})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.827em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">where </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{D}\leq 2\,\bar{\lambda}\,L\,B,\&gt;\&gt;\&gt;\text{where }\bar{\lambda}:=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}\lambda(\sigma_{k}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmlemma4.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma4.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consequently, the expected uniform stability of the DP-diffusion model satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S3.E28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\mathrm{Diff}}\leq\frac{2L_{D}C}{m}\sum_{t=1}^{T_{D}}\alpha_{t}\;\leq\;\frac{4\,\bar{\lambda}\,L\,B\,C}{m}\sum_{t=1}^{T_{D}}\alpha_{t}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>Diff</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></munderover><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></munderover><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\mathrm{Diff}}\leq\frac{2L_{D}C}{m}\sum_{t=1}^{T_{D}}\alpha_{t}\;\leq\;\frac{4\,\bar{\lambda}\,L\,B\,C}{m}\sum_{t=1}^{T_{D}}\alpha_{t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}:=\epsilon_{\theta}(x+\sigma_{k}\epsilon_{k},\sigma_{k},y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}:=\epsilon_{\theta}(x+\sigma_{k}\epsilon_{k},\sigma_{k},y)</annotation></semantics></math>. For each <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, consider the weighted term in the loss,
<math id="S3.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{k}(\theta):=\lambda(\sigma_{k})\,\|f_{\theta}-\epsilon_{k}\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{k}(\theta):=\lambda(\sigma_{k})\,\|f_{\theta}-\epsilon_{k}\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Assume the uniform error bound
<math id="S3.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|f_{\theta}-\epsilon_{k}\|\leq B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|f_{\theta}-\epsilon_{k}\|\leq B</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> and that
<math id="S3.SS4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz in <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> , i.e.,
<math id="S3.SS4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|f_{\theta}-f_{\theta^{\prime}}\|\leq L\|\theta-\theta^{\prime}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|f_{\theta}-f_{\theta^{\prime}}\|\leq L\|\theta-\theta^{\prime}\|</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E29"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\big|\tau_{k}(\theta)-\tau_{k}(\theta^{\prime})\big|=\lambda(\sigma_{k})\,\big|\,\|f_{\theta}-\epsilon_{k}\|^{2}-\|f_{\theta^{\prime}}-\epsilon_{k}\|^{2}\,\big|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.170em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\big|\tau_{k}(\theta)-\tau_{k}(\theta^{\prime})\big|=\lambda(\sigma_{k})\,\big|\,\|f_{\theta}-\epsilon_{k}\|^{2}-\|f_{\theta^{\prime}}-\epsilon_{k}\|^{2}\,\big|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(29)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E30"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\lambda(\sigma_{k})\,\big|\,\langle(f_{\theta}-\epsilon_{k})+(f_{\theta^{\prime}}-\epsilon_{k}),\,(f_{\theta}-\epsilon_{k})-(f_{\theta^{\prime}}-\epsilon_{k})\rangle\,\big|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.170em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\lambda(\sigma_{k})\,\big|\,\langle(f_{\theta}-\epsilon_{k})+(f_{\theta^{\prime}}-\epsilon_{k}),\,(f_{\theta}-\epsilon_{k})-(f_{\theta^{\prime}}-\epsilon_{k})\rangle\,\big|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(30)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E31"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\lambda(\sigma_{k})\,\big|\,\langle f_{\theta}+f_{\theta^{\prime}}-2\epsilon_{k},\;f_{\theta}-f_{\theta^{\prime}}\rangle\,\big|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.170em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\lambda(\sigma_{k})\,\big|\,\langle f_{\theta}+f_{\theta^{\prime}}-2\epsilon_{k},\;f_{\theta}-f_{\theta^{\prime}}\rangle\,\big|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(31)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E32"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\lambda(\sigma_{k})\,(\|f_{\theta}-\epsilon_{k}\|+\|f_{\theta^{\prime}}-\epsilon_{k}\|)\,\|f_{\theta}-f_{\theta^{\prime}}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\lambda(\sigma_{k})\,(\|f_{\theta}-\epsilon_{k}\|+\|f_{\theta^{\prime}}-\epsilon_{k}\|)\,\|f_{\theta}-f_{\theta^{\prime}}\|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(32)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E33"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E33.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\leq 2\,\lambda(\sigma_{k})\,B\,\|f_{\theta}-f_{\theta^{\prime}}\|\quad\text{(uniform error bound)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mtext>(uniform error bound)</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq 2\,\lambda(\sigma_{k})\,B\,\|f_{\theta}-f_{\theta^{\prime}}\|\quad\text{(uniform error bound)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(33)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E34"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E34.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\leq 2\,\lambda(\sigma_{k})\,B\,L\,\|\theta-\theta^{\prime}\|\quad\text{(Lipschitz continuity of }f_{\theta}\text{ in }\theta)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>B</mi><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mtext>(Lipschitz continuity of </mtext><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mtext> in </mtext><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq 2\,\lambda(\sigma_{k})\,B\,L\,\|\theta-\theta^{\prime}\|\quad\text{(Lipschitz continuity of }f_{\theta}\text{ in }\theta).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(34)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">Here, Eq. (<a href="#S3.E32" title="In Proof. ‣ 3.4 Model-Specific Stability Bounds ‣ 3 Stability–Based Analysis of MIA Risk in DP GANs and DP Diffusion Models ‣ On the MIA Vulnerability Gap Between Private GANs and Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">32</span></a>) follows from the Cauchy–Schwarz and the triangle inequality. Therefore, each term in the sum is <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\lambda(\sigma_{k})LB" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\lambda(\sigma_{k})LB</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz in <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, and the average loss over <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1,\dots,K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1,\dots,K</annotation></semantics></math> is:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E35"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|\ell_{D}(\theta;x,y)-\ell_{D}(\theta^{\prime};x,y)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|\ell_{D}(\theta;x,y)-\ell_{D}(\theta^{\prime};x,y)|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E35.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}2\lambda(\sigma_{k})LB\|\theta-\theta^{\prime}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}2\lambda(\sigma_{k})LB\|\theta-\theta^{\prime}\|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(35)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E36"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=2\,\bar{\lambda}\,L\,B\|\theta-\theta^{\prime}\|." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=2\,\bar{\lambda}\,L\,B\|\theta-\theta^{\prime}\|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(36)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text">Applying Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma2" title="Lemma 2 (Uniform stability of functions trained by DP-SGD). ‣ 3.3 Uniform Stability of Functions Trained by DP-SGD ‣ 3 Stability–Based Analysis of MIA Risk in DP GANs and DP Diffusion Models ‣ On the MIA Vulnerability Gap Between Private GANs and Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> with Lipschitz constant <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{D}=2\bar{\lambda}LB" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{D}=2\bar{\lambda}LB</annotation></semantics></math> and total updates <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{D}</annotation></semantics></math> yields:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\mathrm{Diff}}\leq\frac{2L_{D}C}{m}\sum_{t=1}^{T_{D}}\alpha_{t}=\frac{4\bar{\lambda}LBC}{m}\sum_{t=1}^{T_{D}}\alpha_{t}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>Diff</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></munderover><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></munderover><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\mathrm{Diff}}\leq\frac{2L_{D}C}{m}\sum_{t=1}^{T_{D}}\alpha_{t}=\frac{4\bar{\lambda}LBC}{m}\sum_{t=1}^{T_{D}}\alpha_{t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(37)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p4.4.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Why DP-diffusion yields higher membership leakage.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The stability bound from Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma2" title="Lemma 2 (Uniform stability of functions trained by DP-SGD). ‣ 3.3 Uniform Stability of Functions Trained by DP-SGD ‣ 3 Stability–Based Analysis of MIA Risk in DP GANs and DP Diffusion Models ‣ On the MIA Vulnerability Gap Between Private GANs and Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> scales with the product of the loss Lipschitz constant and the total number of DP-SGD steps. For DP-GANs, only the discriminator is trained with a logistic loss that is <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz in parameters. In contrast, diffusion models are trained with a weighted multi-pass EDM loss, where each term is scaled by <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda(\sigma_{k})=\frac{\sigma_{k}^{2}+\sigma_{\text{data}}^{2}}{(\sigma_{k}\sigma_{\text{data}})^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mtext>data</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda(\sigma_{k})=\frac{\sigma_{k}^{2}+\sigma_{\text{data}}^{2}}{(\sigma_{k}\sigma_{\text{data}})^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>. These weights increase rapidly as <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> decreases, amplifying the influence of low-noise terms and leading to a large effective Lipschitz constant. Under a shared network smoothness <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{G}\leq L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{G}\leq L</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{D}\leq 2\bar{\lambda}LB" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{D}\leq 2\bar{\lambda}LB</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\lambda}=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k}\lambda(\sigma_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\lambda}=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k}\lambda(\sigma_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> is typically large. Moreover, diffusion models are typically trained for more steps than GAN discriminators (<math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{D}\gg T_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>≫</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{D}\gg T_{G}</annotation></semantics></math>). Together, these factors imply</p>
<table id="S3.E38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\mathrm{Diff}}\gg\beta_{\mathrm{GAN}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>Diff</mi></msub><mo>≫</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>GAN</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\mathrm{Diff}}\gg\beta_{\mathrm{GAN}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(38)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Applying Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Bound on membership advantage under uniform score stability). ‣ 3.2 Stability Bound on Membership Advantage ‣ 3 Stability–Based Analysis of MIA Risk in DP GANs and DP Diffusion Models ‣ On the MIA Vulnerability Gap Between Private GANs and Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> with a score function of bounded density <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> yields <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{ADV}_{\texttt{MIA}}^{\texttt{GAN}}\leq 2QL_{G}\beta_{\texttt{GAN}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ADV</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">MIA</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">GAN</mtext></msubsup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">GAN</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{ADV}_{\texttt{MIA}}^{\texttt{GAN}}\leq 2QL_{G}\beta_{\texttt{GAN}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{ADV}_{\texttt{MIA}}^{\texttt{Diff}}\leq 2QL_{D}\beta_{\texttt{Diff}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ADV</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">MIA</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">Diff</mtext></msubsup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">Diff</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{ADV}_{\texttt{MIA}}^{\texttt{Diff}}\leq 2QL_{D}\beta_{\texttt{Diff}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since both <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{D}\gg L_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>≫</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{D}\gg L_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{t=1}^{T_{D}}\alpha_{t}\gg\sum_{t=1}^{T_{G}}\alpha_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></msubsup><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≫</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></msubsup><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{t=1}^{T_{D}}\alpha_{t}\gg\sum_{t=1}^{T_{G}}\alpha_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, the upper bound on <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{ADV}_{\texttt{MIA}}^{\texttt{Diff}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ADV</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">MIA</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">Diff</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{ADV}_{\texttt{MIA}}^{\texttt{Diff}}</annotation></semantics></math> is significantly larger than that of <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{ADV}_{\texttt{MIA}}^{\texttt{GAN}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ADV</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">MIA</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">GAN</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{ADV}_{\texttt{MIA}}^{\texttt{GAN}}</annotation></semantics></math>, providing a theoretical explanation of greater membership leakage for DP-diffusion models.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Empirical Analysis of Membership Leakage in DP-GAN and DP-Diffusion</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Building on our theoretical analysis showing that GANs offer greater robustness than diffusion models against membership inference under differential privacy, we empirically assess the extent of membership leakage across both training architectures and a range of privacy budgets. This allows us to assess whether differential privacy mitigates architectural disparities or whether distinct privacy risks remain.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our study compares GANs and diffusion models trained both with and without differential privacy using <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Opacus</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yousefpour et al. 2021</a>)</cite>. We vary the privacy budget <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon\in\{\infty,10,5,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon\in\{\infty,10,5,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, fix <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math>, and apply DP-SGD with per-sample gradient clipping and additive Gaussian noise. Privacy spending is tracked using the Moments Accountant.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">All experiments are conducted on the MNIST dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">LeCun and Cortes 2010</a>)</cite>. To ensure fair comparison, all models share the same optimization settings and training budget. We evaluate sample quality using the Fréchet Inception Distance (FID) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Heusel et al. 2018</a>)</cite>, and assess membership inference vulnerability using standard metrics: accuracy, precision, true positive rate (TPR), false positive rate (FPR), and area under the ROC curve (AUC). Formal definitions of these metrics and implementation details are provided in Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Implementation Details ‣ On the MIA Vulnerability Gap Between Private GANs and Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</p>
</div>
